 "modules": [
  {
   "id": "il03-programming-backbone",
   "code": "IL-03",
   "title": "The Programming Backbone: Python, Java, C++, DSA",
   "summary": "Welcome to IL-03: Programming Language Forge. I'm your instructor, and over four modules we're going to forge the programming backbone every serious engineer stands on — languages as instruments, data structures and algorithms as the canonical craft, and debugging as a discipline. This is Module 1: The Programming Backbone — Python, Java, C++, and DSA. We take the three languages in sequence because each teaches what it's best at: Python for fluency and the standard library, Java for OOP discipline and the type system, C++ for the memory model and zero-cost abstraction. DSA grows alongside — every structure you implement in the language of the week, analyzed from first principles. And throughout it all, Noah is your Socratic debugging coach: hypothesis first, hints second.",
   "objectives": [
    "Write idiomatic code in Python, Java, and C++, translating ideas across all three.",
    "Implement arrays, lists, stacks, queues, trees, heaps, hash tables, and graphs; analyze sorting, divide-and-conquer, and dynamic programming.",
    "Apply SOLID, the working set of design patterns, and refactoring with before/after discipline.",
    "Write correct concurrent code: threads, virtual threads, async/await, locks — and reproduce races before fixing them."
   ],
   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "- **instrument, not identity** — Python, Java, and C++ are tools with strengths; the lesson is choosing the right one for the job, not pledging allegiance to one.\n- **generator** — A Python iterator produced lazily with `yield`; items are computed on demand with O(1) memory, so pipelines can handle inputs that never fit in RAM.\n- **context manager** — The `with`-block protocol (`__enter__`/`__exit__`); guarantees cleanup — files closed, locks released — even when exceptions fly.\n- **duck typing** — Python's protocol-oriented OOP: if it quacks (`__len__`, `__iter__`), treat it as a duck. Dunder methods are the protocols; ABCs (abstract base classes) are the explicit interfaces.\n- **PECS** — Producer Extends, Consumer Super: `? extends T` when reading from a generic (producer), `? super T` when writing into it (consumer). If you can't explain PECS, you don't know Java generics yet.\n- **lazy stream** — Java's intermediate stream ops (filter, map, peek) do nothing until a terminal op (collect, forEach) runs; they fuse and execute per element.\n- **virtual thread** — Java 21's lightweight thread: thread-per-task at massive scale for I/O-bound work. Caveat: `synchronized` blocks pin carriers — prefer `ReentrantLock`.\n- **RAII** — Resource Acquisition Is Initialization: constructors acquire, destructors release; smart pointers (`unique_ptr`, `shared_ptr`) make ownership explicit, so leaks are impossible by construction.\n- **rule of five** — If you define any of destructor, copy/move constructor, copy/move assignment, define (or delete) all five. (Or follow the rule of zero: let the compiler do it.)\n- **move semantics** — `std::move` plus rvalue references eliminate copies; the transfer of ownership instead of duplication is the core of modern C++ performance.\n- **undefined behavior (UB)** — Code the standard doesn't define: use-after-free, data races, signed overflow, uninitialized reads. The compiler owes you nothing afterwards — learn to predict UB from reading code.\n- **Big-O** — A comparative tool, not a prophecy: n log n vs n² at n=10⁶ is the difference between a second and a lifetime. Always ask best/average/worst, and the space-time trade-off.\n- **dynamic programming** — Optimal substructure plus overlapping subproblems: memoize first, tabulate second. Recognize the pattern before reaching for it.\n- **race condition** — Two threads interleaving read-modify-write (`counter += 1` is three bytecodes: LOAD, ADD, STORE) so updates get lost. Reproduce the race first — a fix for a bug you can't reproduce is a guess.\n- **SOLID** — Five design principles for maintainable OOP; the D (Dependency Inversion: depend on abstractions, not concretions) is the seam that makes testing possible.\n- **canonical node** — This module's DSA material is the single canonical algorithms node for the whole university; IL-08 deepens the theory, nothing is duplicated."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "The forge runs on a weekly rhythm: one language, one structure, one race, one pattern. Python week: every script lab from scratch, and you write a failing test *first* — the bug zoo below assumes test-first discipline. Java week: PECS drills until reflexive, then trace one stream pipeline by hand each day, annotating each op LAZY or TERMINAL. C++ week: UB drills — predict the nasal demons before the compiler shows them to you.\n\n1. **Generator pipeline lab** — Build the data pipeline two ways: list-based and generator-based, and measure peak memory with `tracemalloc` on a large input (see scriptLab il03-lab-generators). The required fragments force the laziness comparison.\n2. **PECS exercise** — Write a method taking `List<? extends Number>` (producer: read-only) and one taking `List<? super Integer>` (consumer: safe to add Integer). Get the bounds backwards once on purpose, read the compiler error, fix it.\n3. **Race laboratory** — Write the threaded counter from scriptLab il03-lab-race, run it, and observe the shortfall (expected 400000, got less). Only then fix it with a `Lock`. Show the bug BEFORE the fix.\n4. **BST degeneration** — Implement the binary search tree in scriptLab il03-lab-bst: insert [5,3,7,1,9], verify inorder is sorted (the invariant). Then insert range(1000) sorted and measure the height — 1000, a linked list. That's why AVL and red-black exist.\n5. **Build toolchain** — Compile a two-translation-unit C++ program by hand: compile each to an object file, then link. Watch a header-guard mistake produce a link error, and learn what the linker is telling you.\n6. **Before/after refactoring** — Take one of your own week-one scripts and refactor it against a SOLID checklist (single responsibility first). Keep both versions; the diff is the lesson."
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Your deliverable is a **cross-language kata portfolio**: one problem, solved in all three languages, with analysis. Pick any three of the following — (a) a seeded word-frequency counter over a large text file, (b) an LRU cache with O(1) get/put, (c) Dijkstra over a generated graph, (d) a thread-safe bounded queue, (e) a JSON pretty-printer.\n\nFor each kata you ship four artifacts: the Python solution (idiomatic: comprehensions, generators, context managers), the Java solution (typed: PECS-correct generics, a stream pipeline where it fits), the C++ solution (memory-honest: RAII, no raw `new`/`delete`, move where it pays), and a one-page analysis — Big-O of time and space, where each language helped or hurt, and which solution you'd ship and why. Include the test suite you wrote first and a bug-zoo entry: one bug you hit, the hypothesis you stated to Noah before the fix, and the fix.\n\nThis is the university's canonical DSA node, so the bar is the implementation being *yours*: every structure hand-written once, every analysis done from first principles. Noah grades the reasoning, not just the green tests."
    },
    {
     "title": "Noah check-in",
     "body": "Noah is your Socratic debugging coach for this module — hypothesis first, hints second. Noah probes mastery with questions like:\n- Show Noah your threaded counter shortfall. What's your hypothesis for *where* the interleaving happens — which of the three bytecodes? Don't reach for the lock until you can narrate the race.\n- Trace this stream pipeline by hand, annotating each operation LAZY or TERMINAL, and tell Noah exactly when `filter` first executes: `list.stream().filter(...).map(...).collect(toList())`.\n- Read this C++ snippet and predict: is it UB, and which kind? `int a[2]; a[2] = 5;` — then argue *why* the compiler is allowed to do anything at all.\n- Explain PECS to Noah as if Noah were a junior: why is `List<? super Integer>` safe to *add* Integer to, but not safe to *read* Integer from?\n- *Bug zoo ritual:* before any fix, state your hypothesis out loud. If the hypothesis is wrong, the zoo gets an entry — wrong hypotheses are the tuition you pay for right ones."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: What does this print? xs = [1,2,3]; it = (x*2 for x in xs);…",
     "body": "**Problem.** What does this print? xs = [1,2,3]; it = (x*2 for x in xs); xs.append(4); print(list(it))\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** [2, 4, 6, 8]\n- **Why:** Generators are lazy — the expression evaluates when consumed, seeing the appended 4 (see the generator entry in the Concepts section).\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *Generators are lazy — the expression evaluates when consumed, seeing the appended 4 (see the generator entry in the Concepts section).* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "The forge rewards builders, not readers. These traps stall progress in The Programming Backbone: Python, Java, C++, DSA:\n- **Tutorial hell.** Watching or reading about The Programming Backbone: Python, Java, C++, DSA without writing code. The rule: for every hour of input, produce one hour of output — code, tests, or notes in your own words.\n- **Fighting the compiler instead of reading it.** Error messages are specifications of what went wrong. Read the full message, read it again, then change one thing.\n- **Copy-paste without comprehension.** Pasted code you cannot explain is a liability you installed. If you paste it, rewrite it from memory afterward.\n- **No tests.** 'It ran once' is not 'it works.' A minimal test for each behavior in The Programming Backbone: Python, Java, C++, DSA turns debugging from archaeology into accounting.\n- **Premature abstraction.** Generalizing before you have three concrete cases. Write the duplication first; the abstraction earns its existence.\n- **Language tourism.** Touching five languages shallowly instead of going deep in the module's spine. Depth transfers; tourism doesn't."
    }
   ],
   "cards": [
    {
     "front": "Python: why are generators valuable?",
     "back": "Laziness — they produce items on demand with O(1) memory, enabling infinite/large sequences and pipelined processing."
    },
    {
     "front": "Java PECS?",
     "back": "Producer Extends, Consumer Super: ? extends T when reading, ? super T when writing."
    },
    {
     "front": "Java streams: when does a filter execute?",
     "back": "Only when a terminal operation runs — intermediate ops are lazy and fused per element."
    },
    {
     "front": "C++ RAII in one sentence?",
     "back": "Resource lifetime bound to object lifetime: constructor acquires, destructor releases — no leaks by construction."
    },
    {
     "front": "Rule of five?",
     "back": "If you define any of destructor, copy/move constructor, copy/move assignment — define (or delete) all five."
    },
    {
     "front": "Dijkstra's requirement?",
     "back": "Non-negative edge weights. Negative weights need Bellman-Ford."
    },
    {
     "front": "DP recognition pattern?",
     "back": "Optimal substructure + overlapping subproblems → memoize first, tabulate second."
    },
    {
     "front": "Produce-then-fix: the concurrency lab rule?",
     "back": "First reproduce the race in the sandbox, then fix it — a fix for a bug you can't reproduce is a guess."
    },
    {
     "front": "What does this print? xs = [1,2,3]; it = (x*2 for x in xs); xs.append(4); print(list(it))",
     "back": "**[2, 4, 6, 8]** — Generators are lazy — the expression evaluates when consumed, seeing the appended 4 (see the generator entry in the Concepts section)."
    },
    {
     "front": "Java: List<? super Integer> l — what can you add?",
     "back": "**Integer (safe)** — Consumer super: safe to add Integer; the list holds Integer or a supertype (PECS, from the Concepts section)."
    },
    {
     "front": "C++: which is undefined behavior?",
     "back": "**int a[2]; a[2] = 5;** — Out-of-bounds write — classic undefined behavior; the compiler owes you nothing afterwards (see UB in the Concepts section)."
    },
    {
     "front": "Best worst-case guarantee among quicksort/mergesort/heapsort?",
     "back": "**Heapsort O(n log n)** — Heapsort is O(n log n) worst-case in-place; quicksort degrades to O(n²) without randomization (Big-O, from the Concepts section)."
    }
   ],
