  {
   "id": "il03-systems-software",
   "code": "IL-03",
   "title": "Systems Software: Organization to Software Engineering",
   "summary": "Welcome back to the Forge. I'm your instructor, and this is Module 2: Systems Software — from computer organization to software engineering. Module 1 gave you instruments; this module gives you the substrate every program runs on: how bits become data, how the OS juggles processes, how packets cross the network, how databases keep their promises, and how software gets built by teams. Concepts here become commands in IL-04's Linux path and designs in IL-08's theory track — learn the idea here, type it there. We close with the full-stack capstone: scoped, built, deployed, and presented against a public rubric — the artifact IL-10's career path will present as evidence.",
   "objectives": [
    "Explain data representation, the memory hierarchy, and caching performance effects.",
    "Describe processes, scheduling, memory management, and filesystems as an OS designer would.",
    "Write sockets code; debug networks with packet-level tools.",
    "Design relational schemas, write complex SQL, and reason about transactions and indexes.",
    "Ship a full-stack capstone: scoped, built, deployed, presented."
   ],
   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "- **two's complement** — The signed-integer representation where addition 'just works' and there is a single zero; the reason your CPU doesn't need separate add/subtract circuits.\n- **fetch-decode-execute** — The CPU's heartbeat: fetch the instruction, decode it, execute it — on registers, billions of times per second.\n- **memory hierarchy** — Registers → L1/L2/L3 cache → RAM → SSD: each level bigger and slower. Performance lives or dies here, not in the ALU.\n- **locality** — Temporal (reuse the same data soon) and spatial (use nearby data soon). Caches exploit both; the row-major vs column-major matrix exercise makes a ~10x difference you can measure.\n- **process vs thread** — A process owns an isolated address space; a thread shares its process's address space. Shared memory is the power and the peril.\n- **virtual memory** — The OS's great illusion: every process gets its own address space, and the MMU plus page tables maintain it. A page fault means the MMU found no mapping — the OS pages it in, or kills the process.\n- **starvation** — What happens when a scheduler (FCFS, round-robin, priorities) never gets around to a waiting task; priorities without aging are the classic cause.\n- **inode** — The filesystem's metadata record: permissions, ownership, block pointers — everything about a file except its name.\n- **OSI model** — Seven layers used as a troubleshooting map: when the app fails, work bottom-up (physical → link → network → transport → ...) and find the first broken layer.\n- **TCP vs UDP** — TCP: reliability plus ordering at a latency cost. UDP: speed with app-managed reliability. Chat wants ordering (TCP); games and voice often take UDP with custom recovery.\n- **ACID** — Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability: the transaction contract a database makes with you.\n- **3NF** — Third normal form: every non-key attribute depends on the key, the whole key, and nothing but the key — the rule that eliminates transitive dependencies.\n- **index trade-off** — Indexes speed WHERE/JOIN/ORDER BY on their columns (B-trees underneath) and cost on every write. Design is trade-off; EXPLAIN is the truth serum.\n- **test pyramid** — Many fast unit tests at the base, fewer integration tests, fewest slow end-to-end tests at the top. Invert it and your suite becomes a boat anchor.\n- **REST contract** — Resources modeled as URLs, verbs with semantics (PUT is idempotent — repeating it converges; POST typically creates anew), versioned and documented so a client you didn't write can conform to it."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "This module is a laboratory arc: measure first, then believe. The cache exercise, the sockets series, and the query gym all follow the same ritual — predict, measure, explain.\n\n1. **Cache locality measurement** — Write the matrix traversal twice (row-major and column-major) over a large matrix and time both. The ~10x gap is spatial locality made physical; write down which layout C uses and why it wins.\n2. **Sockets project series** — Build the TCP echo server (blocking), then add `select`, then a threaded version, then the chat server. Feel the C10k problem in your own code before learning its solutions.\n3. **Packet-level debugging** — With the chat server running, debug a deliberately broken client using ping, traceroute, dig, curl, and a packet capture. Read the capture: SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK — the handshake, in the wild.\n4. **Schema design** — Model a small domain (orders, customers, products) to 3NF on paper, then deliberately denormalize one table for read performance and document *why* — denormalize deliberately, never by accident.\n5. **Query gym** — Against a real database, write the graded queries (joins, aggregation, subqueries), run EXPLAIN on each, and add the composite index that kills the full table scan. Record the before/after plans — the decision record is the deliverable, not just the fast query.\n6. **Contract-first API** — Write the API contract first (resources, verbs, auth shape: sessions vs tokens vs OAuth), then implement to it. Your API is graded by a client you didn't write — conformance tests are the specification."
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Your deliverable is the **full-stack capstone**, staged like a real engagement: scoping, implementation, deployment, presentation.\n\n**Scope ruthlessly.** One page: the problem, the user, the three features that matter, and the five you're cutting. The cut list is graded — restraint is the skill.\n\n**Build it end to end.** Frontend (HTML/CSS, JavaScript: DOM, events, fetch), backend API (contract-first: REST resource modeling, auth, versioning, documentation), relational database (3NF schema, indexes justified with EXPLAIN output). The test pyramid governs: unit-heavy, integration in the middle, a thin e2e cap.\n\n**Deploy and present.** Ship it somewhere real, then present against the public rubric: does it run, is the schema sane, is the API contract honored, is the scope honest? This artifact travels with you — IL-10's career path presents it as portfolio evidence, so the README must explain the system to a stranger in five minutes.\n\nAgile practice runs underneath the whole build: a backlog with hygiene, standups with yourself (or your team), and a retro at each stage gate. The SDLC model you pick — waterfall, agile, spiral — must be justified in the scope document: when each fits is part of what you're proving you know."
    },
    {
     "title": "Noah check-in",
     "body": "Noah probes mastery with questions like:\n- Explain your cache-exercise numbers to Noah: why did row-major win, which cache level did the win come from, and what would change the result?\n- Your chat server uses TCP. Argue the UDP case to Noah anyway — what would you have to build yourself to make UDP chat acceptable, and when would it be worth it?\n- Show Noah your EXPLAIN before and after the composite index. What did the planner do differently, and what does the index cost you on every INSERT?\n- Walk Noah through one scheduling scenario: round-robin with a CPU-hog and an interactive task — who starves, and what fixes it?\n- Defend your capstone cut list: which five features died, and what would have broken if you'd kept them?"
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: Row-major vs column-major traversal on a large matrix: the lesson?",
     "body": "**Problem.** Row-major vs column-major traversal on a large matrix: the lesson?\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** cache locality — one can be ~10x faster\n- **Why:** Spatial locality: row-major matches C's layout — the cache-line effect is measurable and huge (see locality in the Concepts section, measured in the Hands-on procedure).\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *Spatial locality: row-major matches C's layout — the cache-line effect is measurable and huge (see locality in the Concepts section, measured in the Hands-on procedure).* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "The forge rewards builders, not readers. These traps stall progress in Systems Software: Organization to Software Engineering:\n- **Tutorial hell.** Watching or reading about Systems Software: Organization to Software Engineering without writing code. The rule: for every hour of input, produce one hour of output — code, tests, or notes in your own words.\n- **Fighting the compiler instead of reading it.** Error messages are specifications of what went wrong. Read the full message, read it again, then change one thing.\n- **Copy-paste without comprehension.** Pasted code you cannot explain is a liability you installed. If you paste it, rewrite it from memory afterward.\n- **No tests.** 'It ran once' is not 'it works.' A minimal test for each behavior in Systems Software: Organization to Software Engineering turns debugging from archaeology into accounting.\n- **Premature abstraction.** Generalizing before you have three concrete cases. Write the duplication first; the abstraction earns its existence.\n- **Language tourism.** Touching five languages shallowly instead of going deep in the module's spine. Depth transfers; tourism doesn't."
    }
   ],
