  {
   "id": "il03-mit-ocw",
   "code": "IL-03",
   "title": "MIT 6-3 Reference Layer",
   "summary": "Welcome to Module 3: the MIT 6-3 Reference Layer. I'm your instructor, and this module is a map, not a territory. MIT's Electrical Engineering & Computer Science curriculum — 6.100A/B Python, 6.1200 discrete math, 6.1210/6.1220 algorithms, 6.1910 computation structures, 6.1800/6.1810 systems, 6.3900 machine learning — distilled into prerequisite order as a study layer over MIT OpenCourseWare's public materials. The platform's rules govern this layer: no duplicated courses (overlap routes to the single canonical node), prerequisite gates before the algorithms and ML nodes, and honest pacing adapted for solo learners. Use it as a reading and problem-set guide; the deep study lives in the IL modules it points to.",
   "objectives": [
    "Sequence the 6-3 curriculum in prerequisite order with a personal study schedule.",
    "Use the 6.100A/B problem sets as the Python practice spine.",
    "Bridge 6.1200's discrete math into IL-02's canonical discrete node — no duplicated courses.",
    "Gate the algorithms and ML nodes on verified prerequisites."
   ],
   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "- **6.100A/B** — Introduction to Computer Science Programming in Python. Its problem sets are the practice spine of this layer: done in order, with Noah for Socratic help when stuck.\n- **6.1200** — Mathematics for Computer Science: proofs and induction, number theory for CS, graph theory, probability for computing. The discrete-math bridge into IL-02's canonical discrete node.\n- **induction template** — Base case, inductive hypothesis, inductive step — until it's muscle memory. This is the proof technique the algorithms courses assume you already own.\n- **number theory for CS** — Modular arithmetic, gcd, Fermat's little theorem: the RSA prerequisites, and the reason 6.1200 matters to security engineers.\n- **6.1210 / 6.1220** — Introduction to / Design and Analysis of Algorithms: sorting, hashing, divide-and-conquer, dynamic programming — gated on discrete-math fluency, because algorithm analysis is applied discrete math.\n- **6.1910** — Computation Structures: the climb from digital logic to the processor, assembly and the machine, caches and virtual memory — the hardware/software interface.\n- **6.1800 / 6.1810** — Computer Systems Engineering / Operating System Engineering: design principles at scale, taught through the classic design papers.\n- **end-to-end argument** — A 6.1800 design-paper staple: place function at the endpoints of the system unless there's a compelling reason not to. Read every design paper asking what problem, what insight, what trade-off.\n- **6.3900** — Introduction to Machine Learning: the on-ramp map, not the territory. The platform runs a prerequisite audit (IL-02 linear algebra + probability) before opening it; the territory is IL-07 and IL-16.\n- **canonical-node rule** — The global rule: where OCW overlaps an IL module (6.1200 vs IL-02's discrete module, 6.1210 vs Module 1's DSA), the platform routes to the single canonical node. No duplicated courses, ever.\n- **term-structure honesty** — OCW problem sets assume MIT's term structure: recitations, office hours, a cohort. Solo learners get adapted pacing — one problem set per week per course is a full-time pace."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "This layer is walked, not watched. The procedure turns six course catalogs into one personal curriculum.\n\n1. **Build the sequence** — Lay out the six courses in prerequisite order (6.100A/B → 6.1200 → 6.1210/1220 → 6.1910 → 6.1800/1810 → 6.3900) and mark every canonical-node overlap: wherever OCW duplicates an IL module, write down the IL node you'll use instead.\n2. **Set the cadence** — One problem set per week per course is full-time pace. Be honest about your hours and pick one course at a time; the schedule is a contract with yourself.\n3. **Run the prerequisite audit** — Before opening 6.1210: can you do an induction proof cold? Before 6.3900: have IL-02's linear-algebra and probability nodes verified? Gates are there to save you from opaque proofs, not to slow you down.\n4. **Work the 6.100A/B problem sets** — In order, with Noah: hypothesis first, hints second. The problem sets are the Python practice spine of Module 1's language work.\n5. **Simulator labs for 6.1910** — Build the ALU in the digital-logic simulator *before* reading about pipelining. The gates-first arc only works if your hands go first.\n6. **Active paper reading** — For each 6.1800/6.1810 design paper, write one paragraph: the problem, the insight, the trade-off. A paper you can't summarize in a paragraph is a paper you haven't read."
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Your deliverable is a **personal 6-3 study plan**: a document that could hand to a stranger and have them walk the layer.\n\nIt contains: the six courses in your chosen order with the canonical-node substitutions marked (which IL modules replace which OCW overlaps, and why); your weekly cadence with the honesty math (hours available, problem sets per week, finish dates); the prerequisite gates you'll clear and how you'll verify each one; and a reading list of five 6.1800/6.1810 design papers with your one-paragraph problem/insight/trade-off summaries for two of them as samples.\n\nThe plan is graded on honesty, not ambition: a sustainable twelve-month plan beats a heroic three-month plan that dies in week four. Include the failure protocol — what you do when you fall two problem sets behind (answer: re-pace, don't cram; the term-structure honesty note applies to your plan too). Revisit the plan monthly; a study plan is a living document or it's fiction."
    },
    {
     "title": "Noah check-in",
     "body": "Noah probes mastery with questions like:\n- State your study order for the six courses and justify each prerequisite edge — why can't 6.1210 come before 6.1200?\n- Do an induction proof cold for Noah (Noah picks the claim). The template should be muscle memory by now.\n- Show Noah your canonical-node substitution map: where does OCW overlap IL-02 and Module 1's DSA, and which node wins each overlap?\n- Summarize one design paper in a single paragraph — problem, insight, trade-off — then defend why the trade-off was the right call.\n- What's your failure protocol? Walk Noah through exactly what happens to the plan when life costs you two weeks."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: Why does the platform gate 6.1210 on discrete math?",
     "body": "**Problem.** Why does the platform gate 6.1210 on discrete math?\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** algorithms proofs need induction and asymptotics fluency\n- **Why:** Algorithm analysis is applied discrete math — without induction and Big-O fluency, the proofs are opaque (see the prerequisite-audit step in the Hands-on procedure).\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *Algorithm analysis is applied discrete math — without induction and Big-O fluency, the proofs are opaque (see the prerequisite-audit step in the Hands-on procedure).* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "The forge rewards builders, not readers. These traps stall progress in MIT 6-3 Reference Layer:\n- **Tutorial hell.** Watching or reading about MIT 6-3 Reference Layer without writing code. The rule: for every hour of input, produce one hour of output — code, tests, or notes in your own words.\n- **Fighting the compiler instead of reading it.** Error messages are specifications of what went wrong. Read the full message, read it again, then change one thing.\n- **Copy-paste without comprehension.** Pasted code you cannot explain is a liability you installed. If you paste it, rewrite it from memory afterward.\n- **No tests.** 'It ran once' is not 'it works.' A minimal test for each behavior in MIT 6-3 Reference Layer turns debugging from archaeology into accounting.\n- **Premature abstraction.** Generalizing before you have three concrete cases. Write the duplication first; the abstraction earns its existence.\n- **Language tourism.** Touching five languages shallowly instead of going deep in the module's spine. Depth transfers; tourism doesn't."
    }
   ],
   "cards": [
    {
     "front": "6-3 study order (6 courses)?",
     "back": "6.100A/B Python → 6.1200 discrete math → 6.1210/1220 algorithms → 6.1910 computation structures → 6.1800/1810 systems → 6.3900 ML."
    },
    {
     "front": "Global rule when OCW overlaps IL-02?",
     "back": "No duplicated courses — route to the single canonical node (IL-02's discrete module)."
    },
