  {
   "id": "il03-generative-packing",
   "code": "IL-03",
   "title": "Generative Art: Circle Packing",
   "summary": "Welcome to the studio — Module 4: Generative Art: Circle Packing. I'm your instructor, and this is where precision becomes aesthetics. We build a circle packer from first principles: rejection sampling with a seeded RNG, then Poisson-disc sampling, then front-chain packing, all accelerated by spatial grids. Then parameters become a design language — Span, Factor, NC, Alpha, AngleX/AngleY, blend modes — each value a deliberate decision. And because we work from inspiration, we close with the ethics: attribution, inspired-by vs copied, and the lab notebook as experimental method. Three studies, one packer, your voice.",
   "objectives": [
    "Implement an honest rejection-sampling circle packer with a seeded RNG.",
    "Upgrade to Poisson-disc and front-chain packing with spatial grids.",
    "Read and write parameter blocks as design decisions.",
    "Keep a lab notebook; attribute inspiration honestly; produce three studies."
   ],
   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "- **rejection sampling** — Propose a random candidate circle; reject it if it collides; repeat. Honest, simple, and slow enough to *feel* the algorithm.\n- **seeded RNG** — The same seed produces the same piece. Reproducibility is the difference between art and accident — and between a debuggable program and a mystery.\n- **Poisson-disc sampling** — Minimum-distance guarantees between samples via the grid-accelerated dart-throwing algorithm: even coverage without clumping.\n- **front-chain packing** — Grow from the boundary inward, placing each new circle tangent to the existing front. Denser, more deliberate compositions than pure rejection.\n- **spatial grid** — Uniform-grid hashing that makes collision checks O(1) amortized: the difference between a sketch and a system.\n- **Span** — The spacing parameter: breathing room vs density. Read it as the piece's lung capacity.\n- **Factor** — The size-falloff parameter: visual hierarchy, large to small.\n- **NC** — Hue layers: palette depth, how many color voices the piece speaks in.\n- **Alpha** — Opacity: the atmosphere and layering depth of the piece.\n- **AngleX / AngleY** — Field tilt: implied light or gravity, the direction the composition leans.\n- **blend mode** — Compositing math (multiply, screen, overlay): how layers converse. Know what the pixels do, not just what looks good.\n- **seeded variation** — Controlled surprise: the seed varies, the design language stays. Same instrument, different performance.\n- **lab notebook** — Seed, parameters, intent, result, next experiment — every study gets an entry. Experimental method applied to aesthetics.\n- **inspired-by vs copied** — Same idea plus your parameters plus your notebook = inspired. Same parameters with no transformation = copied. Lineage is a feature; name it."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Build the packer in layers, and watch the composition quality jump at each one. Keep the lab notebook open from step one — every study gets an entry.\n\n1. **Rejection sampler** — Implement the honest version: seeded RNG, propose a random (x, y, r), reject on collision with any placed circle, repeat until N placed or attempts exhausted. Render it. It will be slow and sparse — that's the baseline.\n2. **Spatial grid** — Add uniform-grid hashing for collision checks (O(1) amortized). Measure the speedup at N=2000; the grid is what turns the sketch into a system.\n3. **Poisson-disc upgrade** — Replace pure rejection with grid-accelerated dart throwing enforcing a minimum distance. Compare the coverage: no more clumping, no more voids.\n4. **Front-chain packing** — Implement boundary-inward growth, each circle tangent to the front. Compare density against Poisson-disc on the same seed.\n5. **Parameter block** — Expose Span, Factor, NC, Alpha, AngleX/AngleY, and blend mode as one block. Read it like a score: each value is a choice about the piece's physics.\n6. **One-parameter experiments** — Change exactly one parameter at a time; record the effect in the notebook (seed, parameters, intent, result, next experiment). Ten experiments minimum before the three studies."
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Your deliverable is **three studies from one packer**: same instrument, three distinct parameter voices — breadth proves the instrument, not the accident.\n\nStudy 1 — *Density*: push Span low and Factor steep; document where the composition breaks (overlap artifacts, muddy blends) and where it sings. Study 2 — *Atmosphere*: Alpha low, blend modes doing the work; the piece should feel like weather. Study 3 — *Tilt*: AngleX/AngleY carrying the composition; implied light or gravity must read at a glance.\n\nEach study ships with its lab-notebook entry (seed, parameters, intent, result, next experiment) and an attribution line: name what inspired it — the Pattlas piece, a parameter block you read, a paper on sampling — explicitly. Inspired-by means your parameters plus your notebook plus your transformation; the lineage section of each entry states what you took and what you changed. Honest attribution is graded alongside the pixels: a beautiful study with a missing lineage fails the ethics bar."
    },
    {
     "title": "Noah check-in",
     "body": "Noah probes mastery with questions like:\n- Show Noah your rejection sampler's collision check. Where's the O(n²) hiding, and exactly how does the spatial grid kill it?\n- Read your own parameter block back to Noah like a score: defend each value as a design decision. Which one would you change first, and what would you expect?\n- Compare your Poisson-disc and front-chain renders on the same seed: which is denser, which is more even, and which would you choose for a poster vs a texture?\n- State the inspired-by vs copied test for your Study 2: what did you take, what did you transform, and where's the lineage recorded?\n- Your notebook's 'next experiment' column: pick the most interesting one and argue why it's the right next move."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: Front-chain packing grows ___.",
     "body": "**Problem.** Front-chain packing grows ___.\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** from the boundary inward, tangent to the front\n- **Why:** Each new circle sits tangent to the existing front — denser, deliberate compositions (front-chain packing, from the Concepts section).\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *Each new circle sits tangent to the existing front — denser, deliberate compositions (front-chain packing, from the Concepts section).* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "The forge rewards builders, not readers. These traps stall progress in Generative Art: Circle Packing:\n- **Tutorial hell.** Watching or reading about Generative Art: Circle Packing without writing code. The rule: for every hour of input, produce one hour of output — code, tests, or notes in your own words.\n- **Fighting the compiler instead of reading it.** Error messages are specifications of what went wrong. Read the full message, read it again, then change one thing.\n- **Copy-paste without comprehension.** Pasted code you cannot explain is a liability you installed. If you paste it, rewrite it from memory afterward.\n- **No tests.** 'It ran once' is not 'it works.' A minimal test for each behavior in Generative Art: Circle Packing turns debugging from archaeology into accounting.\n- **Premature abstraction.** Generalizing before you have three concrete cases. Write the duplication first; the abstraction earns its existence.\n- **Language tourism.** Touching five languages shallowly instead of going deep in the module's spine. Depth transfers; tourism doesn't."
    }
   ],
