  },
  {
   "id": "il03-m36",
   "type": "choice",
   "q": "The spatial grid makes collision checks ___.",
   "choices": [
    "O(1) amortized",
    "O(n²)",
    "O(log n)",
    "free"
   ],
   "answer": 0,
   "explain": "Uniform-grid hashing: each circle checks a bounded neighborhood — the difference between a sketch and a system."
  },
  {
   "id": "il03-m37",
   "type": "code",
   "prompt": "Write iterative binary search: return the index of target in sorted xs, or -1.",
   "starter": "def bsearch(xs, target):\n    pass",
   "required": [
    "while",
    "mid",
    "return -1"
   ],
   "answer": "def bsearch(xs, target):\n    lo, hi = 0, len(xs) - 1\n    while lo <= hi:\n        mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n        if xs[mid] == target:\n            return mid\n        elif xs[mid] < target:\n            lo = mid + 1\n        else:\n            hi = mid - 1\n    return -1",
   "explain": "Halve the range each probe: O(log n). The loop invariant — target in xs[lo..hi] if present — is the whole proof."
  },
  {
   "id": "il03-m38",
   "type": "code",
   "prompt": "Two-sum: return the indices [i, j] of two numbers in xs that add to target (one pass).",
   "starter": "def two_sum(xs, target):\n    pass",
   "required": [
    "target -",
    "seen[",
    "return"
   ],
   "answer": "def two_sum(xs, target):\n    seen = {}\n    for i, x in enumerate(xs):\n        need = target - x\n        if need in seen:\n            return [seen[need], i]\n        seen[x] = i",
   "explain": "Hash map of value → index: for each x, the complement target - x is either seen (done) or stored. O(n) time, O(n) space."
  },
  {
   "id": "il03-m39",
   "type": "code",
   "prompt": "Reverse a singly linked list iteratively (no recursion, no new nodes).",
   "starter": "def reverse(head):\n    pass",
   "required": [
    "prev = None",
    "curr.next = prev",
    "return prev"
   ],
   "answer": "def reverse(head):\n    prev = None\n    curr = head\n    while curr:\n        nxt = curr.next\n        curr.next = prev\n        prev = curr\n        curr = nxt\n    return prev",
   "explain": "Three-pointer walk: save next, flip the link, advance. O(n) time, O(1) space — the pointer-manipulation kata."
  },
  {
   "id": "il03-m40",
   "type": "code",
   "prompt": "C++: sketch a RAII guard owning a FILE* — closed in the destructor, non-copyable.",
   "starter": "class Guard {\n    FILE* f;\n};",
   "required": [
    "~Guard",
    "fclose",
    "= delete"
   ],
   "answer": "class Guard {\n    FILE* f;\npublic:\n    explicit Guard(FILE* f_) : f(f_) {}\n    ~Guard() { if (f) fclose(f); }\n    Guard(const Guard&) = delete;\n    Guard& operator=(const Guard&) = delete;\n};",
   "explain": "RAII + rule of five: the destructor releases, the deleted copies prevent double-close. Scope exit — even via throw — cleans up."
  },
  {
   "id": "il03-m41",
   "type": "code",
   "prompt": "SQL: second-highest salary from employees(name, salary) — no LIMIT/OFFSET.",
   "starter": "-- second-highest salary\nSELECT ",
   "required": [
    "MAX(salary)",
    "WHERE salary <",
    "SELECT MAX"
   ],
   "answer": "SELECT MAX(salary) FROM employees\nWHERE salary < (SELECT MAX(salary) FROM employees);",
   "explain": "The inner query finds the top; the outer finds the max below it. Correlated-subquery thinking without vendor-specific LIMIT."
  },
  {
   "id": "il03-m42",
   "type": "code",
   "prompt": "Java: stream pipeline — names of adults (age ≥ 18), uppercased, collected to a list.",
   "starter": "List<String> adults = people.stream()\n    // ...\n    ;",
   "required": [
    "filter",
    "map",
    "collect"
   ],
   "answer": "List<String> adults = people.stream()\n    .filter(p -> p.age() >= 18)\n    .map(p -> p.name().toUpperCase())\n    .collect(Collectors.toList());",
   "explain": "filter/map are lazy intermediates; collect is the terminal op that runs the pipeline. Annotate LAZY vs TERMINAL when tracing."
  }
 ],
 "cheatSheets": [
  {
   "title": "IL-03 Cross-Language Quick Reference",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "h": "OOP across three",
     "body": "Python: class + dunder protocols, duck typing · Java: class/implements, generics PECS, interfaces · C++: class, virtual, RAII, rule of five"
    },
    {
     "h": "Laziness",
     "body": "Python generators (yield) · Java streams (intermediate ops lazy until terminal) · C++ ranges/views"
    },
    {
     "h": "Concurrency",
     "body": "Python: threading + GIL caveat, asyncio for I/O · Java: virtual threads (Java 21), prefer ReentrantLock over synchronized · C++: std::thread, mutex, atomics"
    },
    {
     "h": "Memory",
     "body": "Python: refcount + GC · Java: GC, escape analysis · C++: RAII, unique/shared_ptr, no GC — you own it"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "IL-03 DSA & Systems Essentials",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "h": "Complexities",
     "body": "Binary search O(log n) · Quicksort avg O(n log n), worst O(n²) · Mergesort O(n log n) · Heapsort O(n log n) in-place · BFS/DFS O(V+E) · Dijkstra O(E log V) (non-negative weights)"
    },
    {
     "h": "Structures",
     "body": "Hash table avg O(1) · BST avg O(log n), worst O(n) · Heap: min/max O(1), insert/extract O(log n) · Trie: prefix O(k)"
    },
    {
     "h": "SQL",
     "body": "Filter nullable side in ON not WHERE · UNION removes dupes, UNION ALL keeps · Index the WHERE/JOIN columns · EXPLAIN before optimizing"
    },
    {
     "h": "OS/networking",
     "body": "Process ≠ thread · Page fault = unmapped address · TCP: reliable+ordered · UDP: fast, app-managed reliability · OSI: troubleshoot bottom-up"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "IL-03 Python Idioms",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "h": "Generators & laziness",
     "body": "Items computed on demand, O(1) memory — pipelines that never fit in RAM.",
     "code": "it = (x*2 for x in huge)\nfor v in it:\n    process(v)  # nothing materialized",
     "lang": "python"
    },
    {
     "h": "Context managers",
     "body": "`with` guarantees `__exit__` runs — files closed, locks released, even on exceptions.",
     "code": "with open('data.txt') as f:\n    rows = f.read()\n# f closed here, always",
     "lang": "python"
    },
    {
     "h": "Comprehensions",
     "body": "Build collections declaratively — but don't nest three deep; readability is the budget.",
     "code": "squares = [x*x for x in range(n) if x % 2 == 0]",
     "lang": "python"
    },
    {
     "h": "Dunder protocols",
     "body": "Duck typing is protocol-shaped: `__len__`, `__iter__`, `__enter__`/`__exit__` are the contracts. ABCs make them explicit."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "IL-03 Java Generics & Streams",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "h": "PECS",
     "body": "Producer Extends, Consumer Super: read from `? extends T`, write into `? super T`.",
     "code": "int sum(List<? extends Number> xs) { ... }\nvoid addInts(List<? super Integer> xs) { xs.add(1); }",
     "lang": "java"
    },
    {
     "h": "Stream laziness",
     "body": "Intermediate ops are lazy — nothing runs until a terminal op arrives. Annotate each op LAZY or TERMINAL when tracing.",
     "code": "xs.stream()\n  .filter(x -> x > 0)   // LAZY\n  .map(x -> x * 2)      // LAZY\n  .collect(toList());   // TERMINAL: now it runs",
     "lang": "java"
    },
    {
     "h": "equals/hashCode contract",
     "body": "Equal objects must hash equal. Override both or neither — HashMap correctness depends on it."
    },
    {
     "h": "Checked vs unchecked",
     "body": "Checked exceptions (IOException) must be declared or caught — the compiler enforces the handling. Unchecked (RuntimeException) need not be."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "IL-03 C++ Memory & UB Rules",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "h": "RAII",
     "body": "Resource lifetime tied to object lifetime — acquisition in the constructor, release in the destructor. No manual cleanup paths.",
     "code": "auto p = std::make_unique<Widget>();\n// freed when p leaves scope — even on throw",
     "lang": "cpp"
    },
    {
     "h": "Rule of five / zero",
     "body": "Manage a resource? Define or delete all five: copy/move ctor, copy/move assign, dtor. Better: compose RAII types and define none (rule of zero).",
     "code": "struct Handle {\n  Handle(const Handle&) = delete;\n  Handle& operator=(const Handle&) = delete;\n  ~Handle(); // defined: we own the resource\n};",
     "lang": "cpp"
    },
    {
     "h": "Move semantics",
     "body": "`std::move` casts to rvalue reference — the move ctor/assign does the transfer. After moving from, the source is valid-but-unspecified.",
     "code": "auto b = std::move(a); // a is now empty-ish; don't use it",
     "lang": "cpp"
    },
    {
     "h": "UB catalog (predict before compiling)",
     "body": "Out-of-bounds access, use-after-free, double-free, signed overflow, uninitialized read, data race, `x++ + ++x`. The compiler owes you nothing after UB.",
     "code": "int a[2]; a[2] = 5;      // UB\ndelete p; *p = 1;         // UB: use-after-free",
     "lang": "cpp"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "IL-03 DSA Patterns",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "h": "Complexity table",
     "body": "Binary search O(log n) · quicksort avg O(n log n), worst O(n²) · mergesort O(n log n)/O(n) space · heapsort O(n log n)/O(1) space · hash table O(1) expected · BFS/DFS O(V+E) · Dijkstra O((V+E) log V), non-negative weights only."
    },
    {
     "h": "Two pointers & sliding window",
     "body": "Sorted-array pair search: pointers from both ends. Subarray constraints: expand right, shrink left while invalid — O(n)."
    },
    {
     "h": "DP recognition",
     "body": "Overlapping subproblems + optimal substructure → memoize first, tabulate second. If subproblems don't overlap, it's divide-and-conquer, not DP."
    },
    {
     "h": "Graph traversals",
     "body": "BFS: fewest edges (unweighted). DFS: connectivity, cycles, topo sort (DAGs only). Dijkstra: weighted, non-negative. Negative weights: Bellman-Ford."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "IL-03 OS & Networking Essentials",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "h": "Process vs thread",
     "body": "Process: isolated address space. Thread: shares its process's space. Shared memory is the power and the peril — guard it."
    },
    {
     "h": "Virtual memory",
     "body": "Every process gets its own address space; MMU + page tables maintain the illusion. Page fault = no mapping found — page in, or kill."
    },
    {
     "h": "TCP vs UDP",
     "body": "TCP: reliability + ordering, latency cost — chat wants this. UDP: speed, app-managed reliability — games/voice take this with custom recovery."
    },
    {
     "h": "OSI troubleshooting",
     "body": "Seven layers as a map: when the app fails, work bottom-up (physical → link → network → transport → …) and find the first broken layer.",
     "code": "ping ok → network layer alive → fault is transport or above",
     "lang": "bash"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "IL-03 SQL & Data Essentials",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "h": "ACID",
     "body": "Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability — the transaction contract. A half-written transfer is a bug, not a feature."
    },
    {
     "h": "Normal forms",
     "body": "1NF: atomic values. 2NF: no partial dependency. 3NF: no transitive dependency — every non-key attribute depends on the key, the whole key, nothing but the key."
    },
    {
     "h": "Index trade-off",
     "body": "Indexes speed WHERE/JOIN/ORDER BY on their columns (B-trees underneath) and tax every write. EXPLAIN is the truth serum.",
     "code": "CREATE INDEX idx_orders_customer ON orders(customer_id);\nEXPLAIN SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = 42;",
     "lang": "sql"
    },
    {
     "h": "JOIN shapes",
     "body": "INNER: matches only. LEFT: all left rows, NULLs where no match — 'customers with no orders' lives here. Know which side keeps its rows."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "answerBank": [
  {
   "q": "Which language should I learn first?",
   "a": "Python — fastest to fluency, gentlest error messages, largest library. Then Java for type discipline, then C++ for the memory model. The order is pedagogical, not tribal.",
   "ref": "il03-programming-backbone"
  },
  {
   "q": "How do I actually get good at DSA?",
   "a": "Implement every structure once by hand, analyze every algorithm's complexity, then do timed problems. The implementation is the understanding; the analysis is the interview skill. IL-08 deepens the theory later.",
   "ref": "il03-programming-backbone"
  },
  {
   "q": "Java streams confuse me — where do I start?",
   "a": "Internalize laziness first: intermediate ops (filter/map/peek) do nothing until a terminal op (collect/forEach) runs. Trace the IL-09 stream lab by hand, annotating LAZY vs TERMINAL per line.",
   "ref": "il03-programming-backbone"
  },
  {
   "q": "Do I need C++?",
   "a": "If you touch systems, games, embedded, high-frequency, or browsers — yes. Otherwise, learn enough to read it: RAII, pointers vs references, and why UB is the exam's favorite trap. The memory honesty transfers everywhere.",
   "ref": "il03-programming-backbone"
  },
  {
   "q": "How do MIT OCW courses fit a solo learner?",
   "a": "Use the 6-3 sequence as a reading/study guide with the adapted pacing in Module 3 — one problem set per week per course is full-time pace. Verify prerequisites (discrete math before algorithms) via the gates.",
   "ref": "il03-mit-ocw"
  },
  {
   "q": "Is generative art 'real' programming?",
   "a": "It's algorithms with aesthetic output: sampling theory, spatial data structures, experimental method. The studio trains precision (parameters as decisions) and documentation (lab notebooks) — both production skills.",
   "ref": "il03-generative-packing"
  }
 ],
